    "narrative": "**Brice Turang** stands out as the clear priority for today\u2019s card, boasting an elite score of 65.44. The data reflects a significant boost tied to the matchup against Roki Sasaki; the combination of the pitcher's slower delivery style and **Brice Turang**'s elite sprint speed creates a high-probability environment for stolen bases, our most coveted metric. While **Travis Bazzana** (56.66) also offers a solid floor against the favorable 4.43 xERA of Montero, he lacks the stolen base upside that elevates **Brice Turang** into our must-start territory. **Seiya Suzuki** (52.65) rounds out the productive core, though his projection remains tethered to the assumption that he retains the #2 spot in the lineup; should that shift, his efficiency score would likely slide closer to marginal territory.\n\nThe decision to cap our lineup at these five reflects the volatility inherent in facing high-tier arms like Gilbert and Fried. **Josh Naylor** (54.98) and **Jose Caballero** (44.61) were the most difficult active roster members to green-light, both battling headwinds from tough opposing starters. **Josh Naylor** is fighting the negative platoon split against Fried, while **Jose Caballero** is essentially a volume play that stays above the bench floor only due to his defensive utility and potential for late-game speed impacts. With **Corbin Carroll**, **James Wood**, **Mickey Moniak**, and **Trevor Story** unavailable due to schedule gaps or injury, the margin for error is razor-thin. We are effectively punting bench depth tonight to protect our limited remaining game caps, opting for the specific speed profiles of **Brice Turang** over fringe contributors like **Zach McKinstry** or **Tyler Soderstrom**, who simply lack the category-specific weight required to justify an active slot on a night with such demanding pitching matchups.",
